# CHAPTER ONE
## HOUSE GUEST
### Part One of Three

---

The shuttle came in low over the smelteries.

Jaina kept her hands flat on her knees and watched the city build itself out of the haze below. Hexani V's capital had no name on her charts. The Alliance survey called it Site Four. The locals, according to a sidebar in Corran's briefing folder, called it Cinderhall, and the brief had not bothered to explain why. Now she could see why. The sky above the city was the color of old solder — yellow at the horizon, grey overhead — and the lower atmosphere wore a permanent slick of refinery smoke that the suns of Hexani's binary system painted gold in the morning. Light slid off it like oil on water.

Below her boots, the deck of the diplomatic shuttle hummed at a pitch she had not heard before. New baffling. New pads. Probably new everything; the Hexani had built this transport to spec for the visit and refused to let an Alliance technician within ten meters of the hull. *Their courtesy*, the message had read. *Our seal.*

"Two minutes," the pilot said over the cabin speaker. Imperial accent. Old Imperial. Coruscanti, but a generation back. "Final descent. Please remain seated, and please do not unsecure your sidearms."

Across the cabin, Colonel Vehk Tarrin pressed his thumb into the bridge of his nose and exhaled. He was Galactic Alliance Diplomatic Corps with two combat tours under the dress collar, and Jaina had liked him within five minutes of meeting him because he had laughed exactly once and only after his aide had stopped looking. He was forty-something, dark-skinned, and he had taken off his rank pins for the trip and not commented on it. Beside him, his attaché — a thin woman named Suren whose datapad had not left her hand since hyperspace — was already drafting something Jaina did not have to read to know was a worst-case report. Suren was the type who finished reports before the meeting started.

Diagonal from Jaina, Jagged Fel sat with his hands folded and his eyes closed. He was not asleep. Jag did not sleep on shuttles. He was doing the thing where he ran through his own briefing a fifth time without moving his lips.

He felt her watching, opened his eyes, and gave her the small grim curl of mouth that meant *I am with you*. It was not a smile. He had not used that face for a smile in almost a year. But it was his, and it was for her, and on a morning like this it was worth something.

The ring on her left hand caught the cabin light. She had taken it off the chain and put it back on the finger for the trip. Jag had not commented on the change. Neither had she. There were certain rooms in which a marriage was a credential and certain rooms in which it was a target, and they had agreed, without agreeing, that this room was the first.

"Anything?" he asked, low.

She let her shoulders ease and reached out, just a little. Not far. Far was a luxury she would not get on this trip.

The Force did not exactly resist her. It pulled.

It was like reaching into a pond and finding the current went the wrong direction. There was no malice in it. No dark. It was simply skewed, the way a level looks skewed against a leaning wall. She withdrew her hand from the pond, and the pond stayed leaning.

"Field's up," she said.

Corran Horn, on her other side, made a sound that was not quite a grunt.

"How big?" he asked.

"City-wide, at least."

He nodded as if she had just told him the time. Corran had been a CorSec officer before he had been a Rogue, and a Rogue before he had been a Jedi, and a Jedi long enough now that the layers of him had settled into a single, sedimentary patience. He had volunteered for this trip the day the request reached the Council. Jaina had not asked why. She suspected the answer was something about Mirax and grandchildren and a desire to put his own body between the worst of this and anybody younger.

He had not brought his lightsaber. Or rather, he had brought his lightsaber, and stowed it visibly on his belt, and not pretended otherwise. The terms of the visit had been clear. *Bring your weapons. We expect nothing less. We will not be insulted by your caution.*

The shuttle banked.

Through the viewport the city slid past in long ribbons: a refinery stack venting orange flame, then a slum belt where the roofs were patched with what looked like deck plating from old Carrack cruisers, then a green parade ground laid out across what had clearly been a strip-mine pit only a few years before. New grass over old wounds. Somebody had planted trees in lines.

Beyond the parade ground, the citadel rose.

It was not a palace. That was the first thing Jaina noticed and the thing she kept noticing for the rest of the morning. It was an Imperial garrison fortress of the late-COMPNOR style, the squat ferrocrete kind built to take orbital fire and shrug. Somebody had built additions onto it the way a poor family adds rooms onto a farmhouse. There was a new comm tower. There was a new landing apron, broader than the old one, and on the apron was a single shuttle of Hexani manufacture, surrounded by two ranked lines of stormtroopers and a single man in dark fatigues, standing apart, hands behind his back.

Even from this height she could see he was not a tall man.

"That him?" Vehk Tarrin asked, low.

"That is an aide," Jag said. "He's not on the apron."

"Good of him."

"It's how he does business." Jag had read the file twice. Jaina had read it three times. They had read each other's marginal notes. "He doesn't perform. We came to him. He will not act like we did."

The shuttle settled. A faint shock through the deck, the cycle of the landing struts, and then the engine note dropped through the floor and away. Quiet.

For a half-second Jaina was aware of her own pulse in her ears.

Suren stopped typing.

"Right," said Vehk Tarrin. "Let's go say hello to the warlord."

---

The ramp came down on cold air that smelled of burnt copper.

That was the first thing — the smell. Not unpleasant, exactly. Industrial. It clung to the inside of her nose the way welding smoke clings, and carried a faint sweetness underneath that she could not place and did not try. Wind moved across the apron from the west, carrying it. Somewhere a klaxon sounded once and was silenced.

She walked down behind Vehk Tarrin, with Jag on her left and Corran a half-step behind. Suren stayed in the shuttle with the comms; that had been agreed in advance. They were four.

The stormtroopers were eighteen, in two rows of nine, with an officer standing slightly forward.

She let her eyes do the work.

The armor was Imperial in the sense that a sword is a knife. The chestplate, pauldrons, helmet — those were recognizably stormtrooper, in white that had been resprayed in matte and weathered down to a working grey. But there were panels she did not recognize. The shoulders were thicker. The lower back held a fist-sized housing she'd never seen on an E-spec trooper. A pencil-thin cable ran up the spine and disappeared under the collar. Personal shield generators. So the rumors had been right.

The rifles were not E-11s.

They were shorter than an E-11 and longer than a carbine, with a heavier emitter and what looked like a heat-sink coiled around the barrel like a wire spring. The stock was straight and unfolded. The optic was a glass eyepiece rather than the old iron sights. It was not a weapon Imperial doctrine had ever fielded. It was the weapon of someone who had looked at the E-11 and decided the E-11 was a museum piece.

"You see the back of the second rank?" Corran murmured.

She did. The trooper third from the left in the second row was carrying something that was not a rifle. It was a flat dorsal pack with two stubby vanes on either side and a cable that ran around to a handheld unit. The unit looked like a thermal detonator that had been cross-bred with a hydrospanner. The trooper had it slung across his front, not pointed at anything, both hands resting on it loosely.

She felt the pond tilt harder when she looked at him.

She did not look at him for long.

"Null array," Jag said, almost without moving his mouth. "Portable. They've miniaturized."

"They've miniaturized a lot of things," Corran murmured back.

The officer stepped forward. He pulled his helmet off in the old Imperial way, one neat motion, and tucked it under his left arm. He was not young. Forty maybe, with the leathered skin of someone who had taken his last sunburn somewhere bright. His hair was Imperial-short. His eyes did not move from Vehk Tarrin's.

"Colonel," he said. "Welcome to Hexani V."

"Captain." Vehk Tarrin was polite. Even.

"Captain Hesh Mallek, Twelfth Garrison." Mallek inclined his head. Not a bow. "Master Solo Fel. Master Horn. Head of State."

To Jag, the salute came almost by reflex. Mallek caught it on the way out and turned it into the same brief inclination. To Jaina he was correct. To Corran he was correct.

"We will escort you to the audience hall. The Lord-Commander will receive you when you are ready."

Vehk Tarrin's right eyebrow moved a quarter-millimeter.

"The Lord-Commander."

"Yes, sir."

"Not the Emperor."

Mallek's mouth did not move. "He does not use that title with off-worlders, sir."

"Ah."

"Sir."

Mallek did not elaborate. He did not have to. Jaina had read the file. The man at the top of this place had at one stage allowed his staff to refer to him as Emperor. Then he had stopped. Then he had ordered the title stricken from internal correspondence. Then he had reportedly broken a stylus on a desk and told a senior aide that styling himself emperor while squatting in a refurbished Moff's office was, quote, the dumbest karking thing he had ever signed off on. The aide had committed all of that to memory and subsequently sold it to Alliance intelligence for a one-way pass to a Mid Rim colony.

That was the trouble with this place. The intelligence on it was either nothing or much too good.

"After you, Captain," Vehk Tarrin said.

Mallek turned. The double line of troopers turned with him, in a single soft scuff of armored boots, and they walked.

Jaina fell in two paces behind Vehk Tarrin and one pace ahead of Jag.

Past her left shoulder, the null-array operator turned his head and watched her go by.

She did not look at him.

The pond, walking, did not get any less crooked.

---

They walked through the citadel for a long time.

She would remember later that her first impression had been *layered.* Not large. Not grand. Layered, the way a wound is layered. The outer corridor was post-Imperial — fresh ferrocrete, neat doors, decent lighting. Twenty meters in, the corridor narrowed and the lighting changed to old-fashioned glow-strips and the walls bore the unmistakable seams of Imperial garrison prefab. Twenty meters past that, a door had been bricked up and a new door cut through next to it, slightly off-square, the new door's frame raw durasteel. There had been graffiti on the wall opposite the new door. Someone had painted over it. The paint had not quite covered it. She could see the curve of an old letter beneath. Aurebesh, but slanted, and not a phrase she knew.

She heard Corran read it under his breath and choose not to share.

People passed them in the corridor.

This was the part she had not expected, and the part she would think about later. People passed them. Two clerks with datapads, ducking sideways into a side passage. A maintenance worker with a shoulder rig of hand tools, who looked at the boots of the front rank of stormtroopers and decided to go the long way around. A junior officer in Hexani brown, who saluted Captain Mallek and then registered the rest of the procession and very visibly stopped breathing for two seconds before recovering and pressing himself flat against the wall to let them past. A woman in civilian clothes — a tunic, a long skirt, a sash across the shoulder that might have meant something here and meant nothing to Jaina — carried a basket of groceries past the mouth of an arch and did not look at them at all.

The civilians did not flinch.

That was the part. They were not afraid. They were busy. They were going somewhere. They were on the inside of this fortress, on the inside of a working morning, with errands. The clerks had stylus marks on their hands. The maintenance worker's collar smelled of grease. The woman with the basket had loaded it carefully — there were two long loaves on the top and a wrapped bundle of greens behind them, and a flask of something tucked along the side where it would not knock against the bread.

This was a place where someone had decided that civilians passing through the work corridors of the garrison was acceptable. That decision said a great deal.

She filed it.

A few corridors on, they passed an open mess. The door was propped with a chair. Inside, two squads of off-duty troopers in undershirts and fatigue pants were eating at long tables, and a server in a sleeveless apron worked a samovar at the back wall. Two of the troopers looked up as the delegation walked past. One of them tracked Corran for a half-second, registered the lightsaber, and returned to his stew. The other watched Jaina go by with an expression she had seen often enough in spaceport cantinas — a measuring look, no hostility in it, that was filing her away with everything else worth knowing.

A holoscreen above the doorway was running a muted broadcast. She caught it in a glance: a man in a hard hat shaking hands with a Hexani officer in front of a half-finished hospital, captions in Aurebesh she did not have time to read. New construction. Public works. The kind of thing a government showed its soldiers when it wanted them to remember why they were eating.

Corran's eyes moved across the same screen. He did not comment.

She filed that too.

They turned a corner into a long broad hall with a vaulted ceiling that had clearly once been a vehicle bay. The floor was sealed and polished and the old paint lines for AT-PT bays were still faintly visible underneath, ghost-tracks of an older Empire. The walls had been hung with banners. Not many. Not ostentatious. Three. Each one bore a sigil that Jaina did not at first recognize and then did: it was the old garrison patch of Hexani V, a hammer and an open hand. The patch had been redesigned. The open hand had been closed into a fist.

"Master Solo," Corran said, very quietly. "Take a look at the ceiling."

She looked. High up, near the apex of the vault, a row of mounting brackets ran the full length of the hall on both sides. There was nothing in the brackets. But the brackets were sized for fixed emplacements and the cabling was new.

"In a parade hall," she said.

"In a parade hall," he agreed.

Captain Mallek did not slow.

At the far end of the hall, two stormtroopers stood at the doors of what had to be the audience chamber. They opened the doors as Mallek approached, without a signal. Either there was an observer behind a peephole or the troopers had been counting footfalls. Jaina would have bet on the second.

The doors opened on a smaller room than she had expected.

She had been ready for a throne. Half-ready. The dossier had not said throne, but every fiber of her experience with men who held this much territory had said throne. There was no throne.

There was a long table. There were chairs. There was, in one corner of the room, a stand with a hookah on it and an ashtray that had not been emptied that morning, and beside the stand a low padded bench in the unmistakable shape of a powerlifting bench, with a barbell racked above it, the bar still loaded with two plates per side. Honest plates. Not props. The knurling on the bar was worn smooth in two places where calloused hands had gripped it again and again.

The walls were bare ferrocrete. There was a long viewport at the far end, which looked out over the parade ground and beyond it to the refinery stacks. The light fell across the table in long stripes.

There were four people in the room.

A woman in Hexani brown stood by the doorway with a datapad, eyes down. An older man with a quartermaster's tabs sat at the table, hands folded. A thin man in a uniform Jaina did not immediately recognize — naval, but not GA, not Imperial Remnant, not Fel — stood at parade rest by the viewport.

And the fourth man was at the table, leaning back in his chair, one boot up on a second chair.

He was the one she did not, at first, fully register.

He was — she had to look twice to be sure — average. Average in a specific and almost difficult way. He was not tall. He was not short. He was very heavily built through the chest and shoulders in the way of men who lifted weights consistently and ate well and did not chase a particular look. His hair was dark and cropped close. He had not shaved that morning, or possibly the morning before. His face was the kind of face she could have walked past in any spaceport in the galaxy without registering the encounter half an hour later. The frown was the only thing about him that seemed permanent. It sat on his mouth like a man bracing for a noise he was not looking forward to.

He was wearing fatigues. Hexani brown trousers, a dark undertunic, a field jacket without insignia. The jacket was open. The sleeves were pushed up. His forearms were a road map of old burns and one long, clean ridge of scar tissue across the back of the left hand that looked surgical.

He did not stand.

He looked at her first. Just at her, for a fraction of a second longer than was polite, and the look was not a leer. It was the look of a man identifying a tool he had heard described and was now seeing in person, and confirming that yes, the descriptions had been accurate. Then his eyes slid past her to Jag, and past Jag to Vehk Tarrin, and past Vehk Tarrin to Corran, and stopped.

"Master Horn," he said. His voice was a working voice. Not deep, not high. A grunt's voice, with a Hexani edge sanded down by years of dealing with people who weren't from here. "I read your account of the Yevethan campaign at the Academy."

"You went to the Academy?" Corran said.

"Pre-coup. Three months. Washed out on the obstacle, I was eighteen and stupid. They made me a trooper instead. Sit down. Please."

He gestured at the chairs across from him.

He had not stood.

He had not, Jaina realized, used a name yet.

They sat.

He looked at all of them, in turn. The frown did not move.

"Right," he said. "House rules. I don't get up. Bad back. Lifted too heavy yesterday and now it's punishing me. If anybody needs to leave the room they raise a hand and the captain by the door walks you out and walks you back; we don't have a problem with that. If anybody wants water, the carafe's on the table. If anybody wants tea, that's coming. If anybody wants something stronger than tea, we'll deal with that after lunch."

He paused.

"Introductions. I'm Jiyad Maerko. Most of the people in this building call me sir. The rest of the galaxy can call me whatever they like. The title my staff prefers is Lord-Commander but I don't care about it. Don't bother with *Emperor*; I cured myself of that one. Don't try *warlord*; I'm tired of that one." He nodded at Vehk Tarrin. "Colonel."

"Sir."

"Head of State." That was for Jag, who inclined his head. "Master Horn." That was for Corran. "Master Solo."

He paused on her, fractionally.

"Master Solo Fel," Jaina said.

He blinked.

It was a very short blink. His head did not move and his frown did not move and his shoulders did not move. He just blinked once, like a man being told something so trivially absurd that he had to give it a full second to make sure he had heard it right.

Then, with no change in expression: "Sure."

She felt him think it. She felt him think *what kind of karking title is that*, very clearly, in the front of his head where he was not putting any effort into hiding it. It was not aimed at her, exactly. It was aimed at the convention that had produced it. The thought passed through him like a hiccough and was gone, and he was already moving on.

She nearly said something. She did not.

Jag, on her left, had taken half a breath. Jag had not missed it either. He was a man trained to register a slight at a hundred meters and decide in the same breath which slights to acknowledge and which to file. He filed it.

"Master Solo Fel," Maerko said again. Even. Polite. With the air of a man repeating a complicated alien word to make sure he had it right. "Welcome to my office."

"Lord-Commander," she said.

"Sir is fine."

"Sir."

He nodded.

"Tea's coming," he said. "Let's wait for the tea. Some of what we have to talk about is going to be easier with something to do with our hands."

The quartermaster at the table — the older man, hands still folded — chose that moment to slide a flimsi folder across the polished surface toward Vehk Tarrin. He did it without speaking. He did not look up. The folder was thin. The corner was marked with a Hexani seal in dull red wax that someone had pressed with a thumb rather than a stamp.

"That's the technical list," Maerko said. "For the prisoners. Read it later. If anything on it makes your stomach turn, cross it out and we'll see what's left."

Vehk Tarrin took the folder. He did not open it.

"Thank you, sir."

"Don't thank me. I'd rather you'd brought what's on the list with you."

The thin man in the unfamiliar uniform — Jaina had decided, watching the angle of his collar and the make of his sidearm, that he was a Hexani naval attaché of some kind, rank unclear — gave Maerko a small look. Maerko caught it and ignored it. The attaché returned his eyes to the viewport. Whatever he had wanted, he was used to not getting it in front of off-worlders.

The room was quiet for the length of a slow breath.

Somewhere on the other side of the door, very faintly, Jaina could hear the rattle of a service trolley.

---

The tea came.

It came on a tray, on a small wheeled cart, pushed by a woman in Hexani brown who could not have been over twenty-eight.

She was thin. She was Mirialan, or part-Mirialan; there were faint geometric markings at the corners of her eyes, but only faint, as if she were second-generation and the family had assimilated. Her hair was cropped short. Her sleeves were rolled to the elbow. She wore a band of plain durasteel around her throat, two fingers thick, with no chain attached to it. The band had a faint matte panel on the side over the carotid artery, and Jaina did not need to be a Jedi to know what that panel was for.

She was a Jedi.

Jaina knew it before she knew anything else, knew it the way you know your own kin in a crowd, knew it across the crooked pond and through the null array somewhere in this building and through the collar and through every effort the woman had clearly made to be small in the room. She was holding herself in. She was not reaching. She was carrying a tray.

Corran's hand, on the table, went still.

The woman set the tray down. She set out cups, one in front of each of them. Her movements were practiced and unhurried. When she set a cup in front of Maerko she did not look at him.

"This is Aris Vael," Maerko said. He said it without ceremony, the way he might have said *this is my quartermaster.* "She was Jedi Knight Aris Vael when we picked her up at Kallos. Now she pours tea. Aris, this is the delegation."

Aris finished pouring. She set the pot down. She straightened.

She looked at Jaina.

The look was a complete sentence. Jaina was not entirely sure what the sentence was. It contained, in roughly equal proportions: *I am still here,* and *do not embarrass me,* and *do not embarrass yourself,* and *we will speak later if there is a later.* Jaina inclined her head a quarter of an inch. Aris's mouth twitched at one corner and returned to neutral.

"Knight Vael," Corran said, evenly.

"Master Horn." Her voice was lower than Jaina had expected. There was a roughness in it that Jaina recognized from her own captures of throat-injured colleagues. Old damage. Healed, but it was there.

"Pour for the Master, please," Maerko said.

Aris poured for Corran. She poured for Jag. She poured for Vehk Tarrin. She poured for Jaina last. When she came around to Jaina's side of the table she stood close enough that her sleeve nearly brushed Jaina's shoulder. The smell of the tea was bergamot and something darker, smoked, that Jaina would not have called tea on any other planet.

"You may go," Maerko said.

"Sir."

"Or you may stay."

Aris paused, holding the pot.

"I'll stay," she said.

"Good. Stand by the side. If I say a thing about you, you may correct me. If a guest says a thing about you, you may correct them, within reason. If I say *out*, you go out. Understood."

"Yes, sir."

"Don't sir me. You haven't sirred me in eight months and you're not starting now."

"Yes, Jiyad."

He nodded.

Aris moved to the side of the room. She set the pot on a sideboard. She did not, Jaina noticed, stand at attention. She stood with her weight on one hip and her arms folded loosely across her front, and she watched Maerko, not the guests.

Vehk Tarrin cleared his throat.

"Sir. With respect. The Alliance has noted, as part of these talks, that we expect the discussion of detained Republic and Alliance personnel — "

"Slaves," Maerko said.

Vehk Tarrin paused.

"Sir."

"Call them what they are. POWs become prisoners become detainees become *personnel* and that's how a man with a clean uniform pretends he didn't know. I keep slaves. You came here knowing I keep slaves. We will get to the slaves. Drink your tea."

He picked up his own cup.

He drank.

After a moment, Vehk Tarrin picked up his cup.

He drank.

---

The tea was good.

That was the first surprise. Jaina had braced, in the back of her head, for the kind of bad caf that warlords served when they wanted to remind diplomats that the warlord did not care about the diplomat's comfort. This was not that. This was somebody's grandmother's brew. The smoke note was perfectly judged. The bergamot did not bully the rest of it. She drank carefully and let it warm her hands.

Across the table Maerko held his cup the wrong way, with two fingers hooked through the handle and the thumb braced against the side. A working man's grip. He drank a mouthful, set the cup down, and looked at Vehk Tarrin.

"All right, Colonel. You came across half the galaxy to see me. What does the Alliance want."

"Sir." Vehk Tarrin set his cup down. "The Alliance, in consultation with the Fel Empire, has come here to seek a durable framework for the cessation of hostilities along the Braxant frontier and the Perlemian approach corridors. Within that framework — "

Maerko had raised one finger.

Vehk Tarrin stopped.

"Try again," Maerko said, gently. "Shorter."

Vehk Tarrin held his face very still.

"What does the Alliance want," Maerko said. "In one sentence. Not in committee voice. I'm not the Senate. I don't take notes."

Vehk Tarrin exhaled slowly through his nose.

"The Alliance wants you to stop expanding, to give back the systems you took since the Verros engagement, and to disband the auxiliary program," he said.

"Better." Maerko drank his tea.

"And?" Vehk Tarrin said.

"And nothing. You asked, I answered. No on the systems. They're mine. I won them with bodies I'm not getting back. No on the auxiliaries. I was a trooper, I still am, and the stormtrooper program isn't something a man in a clean suit gets to legislate out of my hands because it makes his stomach turn. No, generally, on the expanding, but we'll come back to that one because it depends on what *expanding* means in your head versus mine. And — " He turned his cup once on the table, watching the surface of the tea move. " — I will tell you what *I* want."

He looked up.

"I want the Perlemian Trade Route open to my hulls between Kuat and Phorsa Gedd, no tariffs, no inspections, no escort requirements, as far in as the Alliance still controls it. I want the eastern Hydian Way clear from the Bilbringi junction down to the Braxant link. I want the Braxant Run treated as neutral. I want the Triellus corridor opened for civilian shipping, including mine. I want my flag recognized on those lanes. Not as a state. I'm not asking for a senator. I'm asking for a stamp."

He set the cup down.

"That's the actual ask. Everything else is theatre."

Vehk Tarrin had not moved.

"Sir," he said. "Those lanes service — "

"I know what they service."

"Twenty-three Alliance systems draw their primary — "

"I know. I had it counted. I am not asking you to surrender the lanes. I am asking you to take my freighters off the threat board. You don't have to like me to do that. You don't have to call me anything. You just have to stop interdicting traffic that I'm sending through neutral space because somebody on Coruscant has a feeling about my flag."

He drank again. He set the cup down.

"That's what I want. Get out of my way and let me pull this sector together with me on top of it. I don't care about the Core. I never have. I want my freight to move."

He paused.

"And no, I don't like Sith. I want that on the record. I had a Sith try to recruit me eighteen months ago. I sent him back in a series of small boxes. I don't like Jedi either, no offense intended, although there's one in the corner of this room I am, against my own judgment, fond of. I am not a religious problem. I am a geography problem. Solve the geography and you don't have to deal with me."

In the corner of the room, Aris said, without lifting her eyes from her folded arms: "He's also fond of his hookah and the sound of his own voice."

There was a silence.

Maerko's frown deepened.

"Did I ask," he said, "for an editorial."

"No, Jiyad."

"Did I, however, give you permission to correct me within reason."

"Yes, Jiyad."

"Was that within reason."

"Borderline, Jiyad."

"It was not within reason."

"It was true, Jiyad."

He stared at her.

She stared back.

He turned his head back to Vehk Tarrin with an unhurried, almost tectonic motion, as if her presence had personally cost him a vertebra.

"Where was I."

Across the table, Jaina watched Jag set his cup down very carefully so that he would not laugh into it.

---

"You were at *the lanes*," Vehk Tarrin said, mercifully.

"Right. The lanes. I'm not asking for a flag at the table. I'm asking for the Alliance to stop pretending my freighters are pirate hulls. The Fels — " He nodded at Jag, courteously. " — already do me the courtesy of treating my civilian traffic like civilian traffic. I'd like the Alliance to catch up."

"And the prisoners," Vehk Tarrin said.

"What about them."

"There are seventy-two Alliance personnel held — "

"There are a hundred and four Alliance personnel held," Maerko said, mildly. "Your number is from last quarter. We picked up another thirty-two on the Verros withdrawal. Two of those are Jedi. Master Vael will confirm if you'd like. She knew one of them at the academy."

"I confirm," Aris said, from the corner.

"There," said Maerko. "A hundred and four."

Vehk Tarrin pressed the bridge of his nose again.

"The Alliance wishes — "

"You want them back. Sure. You're not getting them all back, but you can have some of them. I'll trade. I will not trade for systems and I won't trade for ships, but I'll trade for things I want, which mostly are technical. There's a list. My quartermaster — " He pointed his cup at the older man at the table, who did not move. " — has it ready. There is, however, the question of Master Vael."

"Sir," Vehk Tarrin said.

Maerko turned in his chair, slow, and looked at her across the room.

"What do I want for Master Vael."

"You want a system, Jiyad," Aris said.

"I want," he agreed, "a system. Cizerack VII. You don't even use it. It's a dirt ball with two functioning hyperjump beacons and a defunct mining lease, and you've had it on the books since the Hapan accession because nobody wanted to be the man who let it go. Give me Cizerack VII and I'll give you back Master Vael."

"I'd rather stay," Aris said, conversationally.

"Quiet."

"I'm pouring more tea." She picked up the pot, came forward, and poured a thumb into Maerko's cup, and then a thumb into Jaina's. As she passed Maerko she said, not under her breath, "Don't sell me for a dirt ball, Jiyad."

"Then I'll sell you for a clean one."

"You sold me for less than a dirt ball, last quarter."

"That was a joke."

"Was it."

"Yes."

"It wasn't funny."

"Most of my jokes aren't. Sit down. Stand up. I don't care. Pour the colonel a fresh cup."

Aris poured Vehk Tarrin a fresh cup. She did it without making eye contact with anyone, but her mouth, very faintly, was working.

"Master Solo Fel," Maerko said.

Jaina turned her head toward him.

"You haven't said anything."

"I'm listening, sir."

"What are you listening for."

"What you want. What you'll accept. What you won't budge on. Whether any of it is a bluff."

He nodded, once.

"And?"

"And I think you mean most of it. I think the bit about Cizerack VII is a bluff. I think you'd take half of Cizerack VII and a tariff waiver and feel like you'd won."

He blinked again.

This time, in the side of his head — she felt this part, faintly, through the crooked pond — there was a brief, surprised pulse of *huh.* Not annoyed. Mildly impressed. Filed.

"That's fair," he said.

"Sir."

"You and your husband can split the difference at the end of the day. I don't mind. I'm here to talk to all of you." He drank again. He set the cup down. He turned the saucer a quarter-turn under it, idly. "Colonel, what else."

Vehk Tarrin opened his mouth.

The door behind them opened.

---

The man who came in was the woman from the doorway's twin, or close enough; another Hexani brown uniform, another datapad, another set of pressed creases. He was sweating slightly across the temples in a way that suggested he had run the last corridor.

He went to Maerko's chair and bent and spoke into his ear.

Maerko's expression did not change at first.

Then it did.

The frown deepened in a way that pulled at the corners of his eyes. His jaw set. He sighed through his nose, a long even outflow. He reached up and pressed two fingers into his temple and ran them in a small circle, the way a man does at the end of a long week.

"When," he said.

The aide murmured.

"How many."

The aide murmured.

"And he took the *what*."

The aide murmured.

There was a sound that, on anyone else, would have been a low growl. On Maerko it came out as a long, weary sigh that had a growl trapped inside it like a fly in honey.

"I'm going to crucify that jumped-up son of a bitch," he said, conversationally, still rubbing his temple. "I'm going to crucify him with his own karking sash."

The aide waited.

Maerko sat with his eyes closed for one full count of three.

Then he leaned to the side and said into the aide's ear, just below the threshold of casual hearing — but Jaina had been a Jedi long enough that *just below the threshold of casual hearing* was, for her, well within the threshold of the rest of it — "Put a team together. I want Brun and Velthi and that lieutenant from Section Four. Hunt him down. Bring him here. And — " He paused. " — when you have him. Cut his sack off and feed it to him. I want it recorded. I want to hear him scream. I want a copy sent to the Margeal envoy with my compliments."

The aide nodded.

The aide stepped back.

Maerko opened his eyes. He looked across the table. He looked at his guests with an air of patient apology, like a host whose pet had just knocked something over in the kitchen.

"Sorry," he said. "Domestic matter."

"Domestic," said Vehk Tarrin, who had also, Jaina suspected, heard most of it.

"A slaver with a name to make hit a depot of mine two days ago. Took weapons, took civilians, took plans I would prefer he had not taken. We'll get him back. We'll get them back. It is the kind of thing that happens out here when you sit on the lanes long enough." He glanced sideways. "Aris. Don't."

In the corner, Aris had not spoken.

"You heard me," he said.

"I didn't say anything."

"You were *about* to."

"I was going to say I hope you don't actually crucify him. The last time you crucified somebody the smell was in the square for a week and Director Vale wouldn't let you do the public-address."

"That was a special case."

"Every case is a special case, Jiyad."

"I told you to stop listening in."

"I'm in the room."

"You're in the corner."

"The corner is in the room."

"If you don't stop, I will have you escorted out."

"If you have me escorted out, I will piss in your teacup the next time I am alone in the kitchen."

There was a beat.

Maerko closed his eyes.

He opened them.

He turned to the technician sweating by the doorway — bald, mid-thirties, a maintenance pin on his collar — who had been valiantly attempting to study the floor and was now, very visibly, suppressing laughter into his closed fist.

"You," Maerko said.

The technician straightened.

"Stop laughing."

"Sir."

"You're not a person. You're a testicle."

Jag, who had brought his fresh cup of tea exactly to his mouth at that moment, made a small sound and put the cup down very carefully without taking the sip. A bead of tea ran down the rim and onto his thumb. He did not move to wipe it. He did not, in fact, move at all for several seconds.

Corran was studying the ceiling with the intent focus of a Jedi engaged in deep meditation.

Vehk Tarrin's face had achieved an absolute stillness that Jaina suspected was the only thing keeping the Alliance Diplomatic Corps from a major procedural incident.

In the corner, Aris turned to the wall and put her forehead very lightly against it.

"Right," said Maerko, to no one and to all of them. He picked up his tea. He drank. He set it down. The frown, which had never really gone away, resettled into its working shape. "Where were we."

"The lanes," said Vehk Tarrin, in the steady voice of a man who had been to worse rooms than this and intended to leave this one upright.

"The lanes," Maerko agreed.

Outside the long viewport, beyond the parade ground, the refinery stacks worked under a yellow sky. Somewhere in the building a door opened and closed. Somewhere in the city, in a place Jaina could not see, a team was being assembled with three names, and someone with a name Jaina did not yet know was, without knowing it yet, already running out of days.

The crooked pond, behind everything, kept tilting.

Jaina set her cup down.

"All right," she said. "The lanes."

---

*End of Part One.*
